From: SacredTrees <121399723+SacredTrees@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 06:10:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Amiga: digests from gfopencrypto.library

CryptoDigestAmiga.cpp (patch 0002) carried its own SHA-256 and SHA-512
round functions and used WTF's SHA-1, so that a digest worked on any task
without a crypto library. The machine has such a library now:
gfopencrypto.library (GFOpenTLS's, installed by GFOpenUp, carried in the
browser's package) takes whole blocks onto a chaining value from any task
(OC_SHA1Blocks, OC_SHA256Blocks, OC_SHA512Blocks) and uses the CPU's
faster paths where it has them.

The file keeps no hash code. It calls four C functions the program that
links WebCore gives: ob_digest_new, ob_digest_update, ob_digest_final and
ob_digest_free (GFOpenBrowser's src/webcore/ob_digest.c, which opens the
library, keeps the part of a block not yet full and pads). One context
serves SHA-1, SHA-256, SHA-384 and SHA-512; SHA-224 stays unsupported as
before.

Without gfopencrypto.library 2.2 or newer a digest gives an empty hash.
An integrity check against it fails, which is the safe side; the browser
does not start without gfopentls.library, which needs the same library,
so a page never meets this.

WTF's own SHA1 and MD5 classes are upstream's and stay: WTF is below any
library call and JavaScriptCore uses them too.

Team 2
---
 .../PAL/pal/crypto/amiga/CryptoDigestAmiga.cpp | 245 +-
 1 file changed, 32 insertions(+), 213 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Source/WebCore/PAL/pal/crypto/amiga/CryptoDigestAmiga.cpp b/Source/WebCore/PAL/pal/crypto/amiga/CryptoDigestAmiga.cpp
--- a/Source/WebCore/PAL/pal/crypto/amiga/CryptoDigestAmiga.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebCore/PAL/pal/crypto/amiga/CryptoDigestAmiga.cpp
@@ -23,236 +23,55 @@
  * ADVISED OF THE POSSIBILITY OF SUCH DAMAGE.
  */
 
-// SHA-1 (WTF's) and SHA-256/384/512 (FIPS 180-4) without a crypto library, so
-// that digests work on any task: AmiSSL wants InitAmiSSL() in every caller.
+// SHA-1 and SHA-256/384/512 from gfopencrypto.library. The program that
+// links WebCore gives the four ob_digest functions (GFOpenBrowser:
+// src/webcore/ob_digest.c, which opens the library and pads); there is no
+// hash code here. Any task may call them. Without the library a digest
+// gives an empty hash, which matches nothing.
 
 #include "config.h"
 #include "CryptoDigest.h"
 
-#include <array>
-#include <wtf/SHA1.h>
+extern "C" {
+void* ob_digest_new(int kind);
+void ob_digest_update(void* digest, const void* data, unsigned long length);
+int ob_digest_final(void* digest, unsigned char* out);
+void ob_digest_free(void* digest);
+}
 
